contentText:
Thanks to being half of Tears For Fears , Roland Orzabal has certainly had a life and career to shout about. But doing that himself was not really on his bingo card.

Related

Tears for Fears Is on a Creative High: &#8216;We Still Have a Desire to Get&nbsp;Better&#8217;

Fans Choose Ariana Grande&#039;s &#039;Petal&#039; as This Week&#039;s Favorite New Music

Ariana Grande Taking a Break After Tour Amid Ongoing Public Scrutiny

Orzabal &#8212; whose Welcome to Your Life: Love, Death and Tears For Fears (Dey Street) publishes on Aug. 4 &#8212; had no desire to write a memoir. He&#8217;d written a novel, 2014&#8217;s Sex, Drugs & Opera, and was, in fact, shopping another one, Truthwall , when his literary agent Mark Gottlieb broached the autobiography idea.

&#8220;Every time he took (the new novel) to a publisher, they would say, &#8216;What about a memoir?'&#8221; the British-born Orzabal, 64, tells Billboard via Zoom from his current home on the south coast of Massachusetts. &#8220;So he said, &#8216;Have you ever thought about writing a memoir?&#8217; And I said, &#8216;No, I haven&#8217;t.&#8217; But I always wanted to write about astrology because I&#8217;ve been studying it for 40 years, and I always wanted to write about metaphysics, my philosophy, my take on life, my understanding of the human condition. So I thought maybe I could combine the two and explain my life astrologically &#8212; along with Tears For Fears, obviously. And Mark said to me, &#8216;Just write three or four chapters and leave it with me.'&#8221;

The result is a 268-page tome that Orzabal further subtitled An Astrological Memoir , with a foreword dubbed &#8220;Warning: This Book Contains Astrology&#8221; to explain his fascination and introduce the concept. Welcome To Your Life makes plenty of references to star patterns and Zodiac signs in explaining some of the events in Orzabal&#8217;s life, but he worked hard to make sure that never interferes with the narrative readers come for.

&#8220;My initial feeling was to weave it in like a tapestry,&#8221; Orzabal explains, but his publisher, Carrie Thornton, instead recommended he &#8220;put it in as interstitials at the end of a chapter, so people can read the story and ignore the astrology. I understand that completely. The astrology is another layer of explanation, and once I attempted it a few times I thought, &#8216;Yeah, this is brilliant.&#8217; I spread it throughout the book, so it becomes more understandable, and if someone doesn&#8217;t want to get into it, it&#8217;s easy enough to skip it.&#8221;

Let It All Out

His bandmate Curt Smith apparently felt that way when Orzabal sent him an early edition of the memoir. &#8220;He texted me or emailed me and he&#8217;s just finished it, and he was in tears, so I thought that was a good sign,&#8221; Orzabal recalls. &#8220;And then I said to him, &#8216;What do you think of the astrology?&#8217; and he said, &#8216;Oh, I didn&#8217;t read that,&#8217; or &#8216;I didn&#8217;t understand it.&#8217; [Laughs] So there you go. Curt hasn&#8217;t seen (the book) since, but I&#8217;m sure he&#8217;ll find it more accessible now.&#8221;

Welcome To Your Life offers a deep and earnest dive into Orzabal&#8217;s life, from a difficult upbringing (his parents divorced when Orzabal was a teen) through his youth in Bath, England and his developing passion for arts. He and Smith met during their early teens and formed a band called Graduate, which released one album and broke up, leading to Tears For Fears. The two developed a deep interest in psychologist Arthur Janov and his primal scream therapy, which informed songs on Tears for Fears’ 1983 debut The Hurting and 1985&#8217;s Billboard 200-topping Songs From the Big Chair, which spawned two Billboard Hot 100 No. 1s.

&#8220;We were young and evangelical,&#8221; Orzabal says. &#8220;We believed (primal scream) to be the absolute truth…So no one ever understood us. We weren&#8217;t ever understood by journalists &#8217;cause we started off, as it says in the book, talking about Janov. The Hurting was a good body of work, and it bled into the massive success of Songs From the Big Chair . Then the thing that inspired us, that made us completely different to every other band, was forgotten &#8212; which is fine.

&#8220;So in (the book), I wanted to fully explain that, because it was very much what set Curt and I apart from the music scene. There&#8217;s a chapter called &#8216;A Process of Becoming,&#8217; which is the story of primal therapy. It&#8217;s a lovely story.&#8221;

Welcome To Your Life also delves into the dynamics of the Orzabal-Smith relationship and their 1991 split, with Orzabal retaining the band name and recording two more TFF albums as well as a solo album, Tomcats Screaming Outside , in 2001, before they reunited around the same time. Despite disagreements, particularly over management during the late ‘80s, Orzabal says he had no desire to make Smith a villain at any point of the story.

&#8220;Had I written the book in the ‘90s, he&#8217;d definitely be (a villain),&#8221; says Orzabal, who also writes candidly about his late first wife Caroline&#8217;s struggle with depression, substance abuse, dementia and cirrhosis before her passing in 2017. (He remarried in 2020 and has a one-year-old daughter in addition to two adult sons from his first marriage.) &#8220;We had a bit of money after Big Chair , and even after The Hurting. I think Curt realized the change of status quite hugely; he recognized he was a pop star, and I didn&#8217;t. He bought a massive house in the south of Bath and he lived the life of the nouveau riche . I had other priorities.

&#8220;The thing is, (Smith) is a very good partner, and we have a very good balance and I don&#8217;t necessarily want that to end. He&#8217;s seen me through some very, very bad times and some ugly times, and he&#8217;s one of these people that doesn&#8217;t panic. And we are in our mid-sixties &#8212; he&#8217;s 65, I&#8217;m about to be 65 [on Aug. 22]. I think the relationships you have that have lasted decades, albeit with their ups and downs, become so precious as we get closer to the D-Day, so you appreciate them that much more.&#8221;

Head Over Heels

Rather than feeling like a rock star in TFF, Orzabal says now that, &#8220;I always felt like I was part of something. I never felt that I was the center of it. There are some moments I describe in the book…like in the Big Chair days we were in Orange County and we had helicopters coming over the brow of the hill, shining lights on people and &#8216;Listen&#8217; was playing on the PA and I just thought, &#8216;This is incredible &#8212; not because of me, but because I&#8217;m part of something which has taken off like this.&#8217;

&#8220;I still don&#8217;t think about (stardom), really at all, which is a good thing. The other day I was in the garden center here and I&#8217;m cashing out with some plants and a woman said, &#8216;I&#8217;m a big fan.&#8217; And I thought, &#8216;Of the plants?&#8217; I forget, because I&#8217;m thinking about other things.&#8221;

Orzabal is nevertheless hopeful that Welcome To Your Life will shine a bit of light on some of the overlooked music, especially the two non-Smith TFF albums and his solo effort &#8212; perhaps even back to Graduate. &#8220;I hope that people will go and listen to anything, really,&#8221; he says. &#8220;When I read Bono&#8217;s book ( Surrender: 40 Songs, One Story ) he mentions a lot of songs that I really didn&#8217;t know about, so I went back and listened to them as a point of reference, just to make myself more knowledgeable.&#8221;

Welcome To Your Life has its rock star moments, however. One anecdote shares that the late Joe Strummer admonished Orzabal that he &#8220;owes me a fiver&#8221; because the phrase &#8220;everybody wants to rule the world&#8221; appears in the Clash&#8217;s 1980 song &#8220;Charlie Don&#8217;t Surf&#8221;; Orzaba…
